## Program

Create a Python file named `anonymization.py`.

```python
import pandas as pd
import numpy as np

# Sample Dataset
data = {
    "Name": ["Aman", "Riya", "Rahul", "Priya", "Karan", "Neha"],
    "Age": [21, 22, 21, 22, 35, 36],
    "City": ["Delhi", "Delhi", "Delhi", "Delhi", "Mumbai", "Mumbai"],
    "Email": [
        "aman@gmail.com",
        "riya@gmail.com",
        "rahul@gmail.com",
        "priya@gmail.com",
        "karan@gmail.com",
        "neha@gmail.com"
    ]
}

df = pd.DataFrame(data)

print("Original Dataset:")
print(df)

# 1. Data Masking
masked_df = df.copy()

masked_df["Name"] = masked_df["Name"].str[0] + "***"
masked_df["Email"] = masked_df["Email"].str[0] + "***@gmail.com"

print("\nData Masking:")
print(masked_df)

# 2. K-Anonymity (K=2)
k_df = df.copy()

k_df["Age"] = (k_df["Age"] // 10) * 10
k_df["Name"] = "***"
k_df["Email"] = "***"

print("\nK-Anonymity:")
print(k_df)

# 3. Differential Privacy (Illustration)
true_count = len(df)
epsilon = 1.0

noise = np.random.laplace(0, 1 / epsilon)
private_count = true_count + noise

print("\nDifferential Privacy:")
print("Actual Count:", true_count)
print("Noisy Count:", round(private_count, 2))
```

## Procedure
1. Import Pandas and NumPy libraries.
2. Create a sample dataset containing personal information.
3. Apply data masking to hide names and email addresses.
4. Apply K-Anonymity by generalizing age and removing direct identifiers.
5. Apply differential privacy by adding Laplace noise to the dataset count.
6. Execute the program and observe the results.

